# Example Caddy configuration for ForgeDB's public HTTP entrypoint. See
# docs/deployment/phase15-leader-aware-gateway.md for the full design
# writeup of why this file looks the way it does.
#
# THE PROBLEM THIS FILE FIXES
#
# Pointing Caddy straight at one ForgeDB node's address pins every
# public request to whichever node happened to be leader when this file
# was written:
#
#   example.com {
#       reverse_proxy forgedb-2:8080
#   }
#
# Raft leadership moves on failure/election; Caddy has no way to notice
# -- a 421 Misdirected Request is a perfectly valid HTTP response as far
# as Caddy is concerned, not a backend failure, so Caddy's own passive
# health checks never react to it, and Caddy has no way to parse the
# response body's leader_http hint (see internal/api/kv.go) and retry
# elsewhere even if it wanted to. Every write and linearizable read
# starts failing with 421 the moment that node stops being leader, until
# an operator manually edits this file to point at the new one.
#
# THE FIX
#
# Point Caddy at forge-gateway instead of any single node.
# forge-gateway (cmd/forge-gateway / internal/gateway) is a small,
# persistent reverse proxy that already speaks the not-leader hint
# protocol every ForgeDB node answers with -- it forwards each request
# to whichever node currently claims to be leader, follows that node's
# hint when it is wrong, and only answers the client once a node has
# actually accepted the request. forge-gateway's own address never
# changes across a Raft election -- only which node it is currently
# forwarding to does -- so this file never needs to change when
# leadership moves.
#
# {$FORGEDB_PUBLIC_HOSTNAME} is Caddy's own environment-variable
# placeholder syntax (not a ForgeDB feature) -- Caddy substitutes it
# with the FORGEDB_PUBLIC_HOSTNAME environment variable's value at
# startup, before parsing the rest of this file, so the real public
# hostname never has to be written into this tracked file (see
# deploy/caddy.env.example for how to set it on the Azure VM). Once
# this resolves to a real DNS hostname (anything other than
# "localhost" or a bare IP), Caddy's automatic HTTPS turns on for this
# site with no further configuration here: it requests a certificate
# from Let's Encrypt via the ACME HTTP-01 challenge (needs port 80
# reachable from the Internet, briefly, per issuance/renewal), listens
# on :443, and redirects plain :80 requests to :443. See
# deploy/README.md's HTTPS section for the DNS/NSG prerequisites and
# how to validate the certificate once it's issued.
{$FORGEDB_PUBLIC_HOSTNAME} {
	# Baseline security headers (Phase 13 --
	# docs/deployment/phase13-hardening.md). These are static response
	# headers Caddy adds to every response regardless of upstream status,
	# and are additive to whatever forge-gateway/a ForgeDB node already
	# set (including CORS's Access-Control-* headers and /kv's
	# application/octet-stream bodies -- see internal/api/cors.go and
	# internal/api/kv.go) -- Caddy's `header` directive sets/adds named
	# headers, it does not replace or strip any others:
	#   - X-Content-Type-Options: stops a browser from "sniffing" a
	#     response's declared Content-Type into something else, which
	#     matters here because /kv GET responses are
	#     application/octet-stream and /metrics is a bare text format.
	#   - X-Frame-Options: refuses to let this site be framed by another
	#     origin (clickjacking defense); the API/dashboard have no
	#     legitimate reason to be embedded in a frame.
	#   - Referrer-Policy: never sends this site's full URL (which could
	#     include a /kv/{key} path) as a Referer header to a different
	#     origin.
	# None of these affect request routing, the streaming/binary /kv
	# response bodies, or /health -- they are response headers only.
	header {
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		X-Frame-Options "DENY"
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
	}

	# forge-gateway:8090 (the Docker Compose service name) only resolves
	# on forgedb-net, the Docker-internal network -- Caddy itself runs
	# natively on the VM, outside that network (see docker-compose.yml's
	# forge-gateway service comment), and reaches forge-gateway the same
	# way deploy/README.md's curl examples do: via the loopback-bound
	# published port.
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8090
}

# forge-gateway forwards Authorization headers unchanged and does not
# itself require FORGEDB_API_TOKEN (see docker-compose.yml's
# forge-gateway service) -- every protected endpoint's authentication is
# still enforced exactly as before, inside whichever ForgeDB node
# ultimately answers (see internal/api/auth.go). This is true
# regardless of what sits in front of Caddy: a stable DNS hostname with
# Caddy terminating TLS directly (the intended production setup -- see
# deploy/README.md's HTTPS section), or a temporary Cloudflare Quick
# Tunnel in front of plain HTTP Caddy (useful for local development/
# testing, but not a stable production endpoint -- its hostname changes
# every restart). Either way, only the reverse_proxy upstream target
# matters here: a specific node vs. the stable gateway address.
#
# CORS is NOT handled here. The dashboard's cross-origin access (see
# docs/deployment/phase13-hardening.md) is handled end to end by
# internal/api/cors.go, configured via FORGEDB_CORS_ORIGINS on each
# ForgeDB node -- forge-gateway transparently relays whatever
# Access-Control-* headers that node's response already carries (see
# internal/gateway/proxy.go's writeResponse), and this Caddyfile adds
# none of its own, so there is exactly one place CORS policy is ever
# decided.
